          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p Traditionally the Cantor space was conceived of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AsSubspaceOfTheRealLine"} as a subspace of the real line
          %p but of course it may also be described
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AsAbstractTopologicalSpace"} as an abstract space
          %p in itself.
          %h3#AsAbstractTopologicalSpace As an abstract space
          %p
            In brief, Cantor space may be abstractly described as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+product+space"} topological product space
            of countable many copies of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0, 1\}
          %p
            Recall that a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+digit"} binary digit
            is either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            or
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+domain"} Boolean domain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔹
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}
          %p
            A
            %strong point
            in Cantor space is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+sequence"} infinite sequence
            of binary digits. Accordingly, Cantor space may be denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{N}}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
          %p
            An
            %strong open
            in Cantor space is a collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} finite sequences
            of binary digits (that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monoid"} free monoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^*
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in G
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
                is an extension of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                (that is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                with possibly additional digits added to the end), then
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in G
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u:0 \in G
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u:1 \in G
                (where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u:i
                is the immediate extension of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                by the digit
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in G
          %p
            A point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %strong belongs
            to an open
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            if, for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is an extension of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
          %p
            An alternative characterization of Cantor space is as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra+for+an+endofunctor"} terminal coalgebra
            for the endofunctor on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto X + X
          %h3#what_kind_of_space What kind of space?
          %p
            Traditionally, Cantor space is understood as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as defined above.
          %p
            A newer approach is to understand Cantor space as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order"} order
            relation on them to define a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \leq H
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \subseteq H
            as subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+locale"} points
            come for free, and correspond precisely to the points as defined above.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+space"} sober
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalisation"} internal
            to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponentiable
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            mathematics. However, the theorem that an open is determined by its points (so that Cantor space, as a locale, is
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+locale"} topological
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
          %p When the fan theorem is not valid, the localic approach is probably better; it allows more of the useful properties of Cantor space to hold.
          %h3#AsSubspaceOfTheRealLine As a subspace of the real line
          %p
            Cantor space is usually conceived of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(\{0,1\})
            for the the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+space"} discrete topological space
            with two points. Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\prod} Disc(\{0,1\})
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable set
            of copies of this discrete space with itself (i.e. the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\prod} \{0,1\}
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+topology"} Tychonoff topology
            induced from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
          %p
            Then consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi ℕ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi κ
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi ℕ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ↦
                            %mphantom
                              %mi AAAA
                        %mtd
                          %munderover
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo =
                              %mn 1
                            %mn ∞
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mn 3
                                %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\prod}
                  \&amp;\overset{\kappa}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                  [0,1]
                  \\\
                  (a_i)_{i \in \mathbb{N}}
                  \&amp;\overset{\phantom{AAAA}}{\mapsto}&amp;
                  \\underoverset{i = 1}{\infty}{\sum} \frac{2 a_i}{3^i}
                  }
          %p
            which sends an element in the product space, hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of binary digits, to the value of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            as shown on the right.
          %p
            One checks that this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            (from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topology"} product topology
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
            on the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa\left( \underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\prod} \{0,1\}\right) \subset [0,1]
            equipped with its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            onto its image:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mphantom
                        %mi AA
                      %mo ≃
                      %mphantom
                        %mi AA
                  %mi κ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAA
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\prod} Disc(\{0,1\})
                  \\overset{\phantom{AA}\simeq\phantom{AA}}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\kappa\left(
                  \\underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\prod} Disc(\{0,1\})
                  \\right)
                  \\overset{\phantom{AAAA}}{\hookrightarrow}
                  [0,1]
                  \\,.
          %p
            This image is the
            %em Cantor space
            as a subspace of the closed interval.
          %p
            From the localic perspective, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of frames in the opposite direction. Given an open
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            (as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in Cantor space such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in G
            if and only if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi len
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 3
                        %mi i
                  %mo ∼
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi len
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 3
                        %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 3
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi len
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi u
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i=1}^{len(u)} \frac { 2 u_i } { 3^i } \sim \sum_{i=1}^{len(u)} \frac { 2 u_i } { 3^i } + \frac 1 { 3^{-len(u)} } .
          %p One then checks that this is an embedding.
          .query
            %p I should check this some day; for the moment, I am taking it on faith. —Toby
          %p In either case, the idea is:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A point of Cantor space corresponds to a number written in base
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
                with infinitely many digits, using only the digits
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                (which are the options for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 a_i
                when
                = succeed ");" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i \in \{0,1\}
            %li
              %p
                An open corresponds to a union of intervals, each of which is given by approximating a number in base
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
                to a finite number of digits, using only the digits
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            One sometimes speaks of the
            %strong Cantor set
            to stress that one is considering Cantor space as a subspace of the real line.
          %p
            As we can also consider Cantor space as a product space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{Z}/2)^n
            of countably many copies of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/(2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+space"} homogeneous space
            (its group of self-homeomorphisms acts transitively on the space).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Cantor space, especially in its guise as a subspace of the real line, is quite famous; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor_set"} Wikipedia
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Cantor space is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+space"} compact Hausdorff space
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
            %li
              %p
                Cantor space is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} totally disconnected
            %li
              %p
                Thus Cantor space is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+space"} Stone space
            %li
              %p
                Cantor space is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metrizable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
                of Cantor space (see Theorem
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HA"}
                below).
            %li
              %p
                As a subspace of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+space"} perfect
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uncountable+set"} uncountable
                but of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+set"} measure zero
            %li
              %p
                The Cantor set is a precisely self-similar
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractal"} fractal
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+dimension"} Hausdorff dimension
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi log
                          %mn 3
                        %mn 2
                        %mo ≈
                        %mn 0.631
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log_3 2 \approx 0.631
          #Theorem.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
              to Cantor space if and only if it is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} nonempty
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+disconnected+topological+space"} totally disconnected
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metrizable
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+topological+space"} perfect
          %p
            This result is sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer"}> Brouwer
                ‘s theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom"} atomless
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            are
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/back-and-forth+argument"} back-and-forth argument
          #CorA.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{X}
              of a space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              that is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second-countable+space"} second-countable
              %span.newWikiWord
                locally compact
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/local+compact+space"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+disconnected+space"} totally disconnected
              and perfect, is homeomorphic to Cantor space (provided
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is not itself compact).
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{X}
              is also second-countable, compact Hausdorff and therefore compact regular, and so by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urysohn+metrization+theorem"} Urysohn metrization theorem
              it is compact metrizable. The point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              at infinity is not isolated since we assume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is not compact, so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{X}
              is perfect. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is any open neighborhood of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \neg K
              for some compact
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \subset X
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              contains a clopen whence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p\}
              is the quasi-component of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              (hence also the connected component since we’re in a compact Hausdorff space). But the argument
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+rings+are+profinite#compopen"} here
              shows that for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in K
              there is a clopen neighborhood of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              contained in
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \{p\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            It follows from this result that all such spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are homeomorphic: they all have Cantor space as their one-point compactifications, and so they are all homeomorphic to the space obtained obtained by removing a single point from Cantor space. This applies for example to spaces obtained by removing a finite number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            of points from Cantor space.
          %p Cantor space is also a “universal” compact metric space in the following sense.
          #HA.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Hausdorff-Alexandroff)
              Every compact metric space is a continuous image of Cantor space.
          %p
            This implies that every compact metric space is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
            of Cantor space, since a surjective map between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+spaces"} compact Hausdorff spaces
            is a closed surjection, and closed surjections are quotient maps.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              First, every compact metric space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+set"} dense set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_0, x_1, \ldots\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d: X \times X \to [0, \infty)
              is valued in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y: X \to [0, 1]^\mathbb{N}
              to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+cube"} Hilbert cube
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y(x) = (d(x, x_n))_{n \in \mathbb{N}}
            %p
              (a type of restricted “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"}> Yoneda embedding
              ”, regarding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
              as
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              homeomorphically onto a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed subspace
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^\mathbb{N}
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Peano+curve#construction"} Peano curve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to I
              from Cantor space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = 2^\mathbb{N}
              (Cantor-Lebesgue function), and there is a homeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo ×
                        %mi ℕ
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo ;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = 2^\mathbb{N} \cong 2^{\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}} \cong (2^\mathbb{N})^\mathbb{N} = C^\mathbb{N};
            %p
              therefore there is a continuous surjection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ≅
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \cong C^\mathbb{N} \to I^\mathbb{N}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↪
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi surj
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi surj
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ↪
                              %mi y
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi I
                              %mi ℕ
                            %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K &amp; \hookrightarrow &amp; C \\
                    \\mathllap{surj} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{surj} \\
                    X &amp; \stackrel{y}{\hookrightarrow} &amp; I^\mathbb{N},
                    }
            %p
              we see that to produce a continuous surjection
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to K
            %p
              In fact, every closed subspace
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \hookrightarrow C
              admits a retraction. There is a clever trick for seeing this: represent Cantor space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              instead as the subspace of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              whose points, when written in base
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 6
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 6
              = succeed "'s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "'s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in C
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo +
                          %mi y
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{x+y}{2} \notin C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z \in C
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) = d(x, z)
              only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = z
              and so: given a closed subspace
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in C
              a
              %em unique
              element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_x \in K
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, k_x) = d(x, K)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto k_x
              is continuous (in fact a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+function"} locally constant function
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∖
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \setminus K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              as is easily seen) and provides the desired retraction.
